/*! \page developer_template_div_page Standard vs. Divided Templates

\ref developer_template_defs_page  (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_param_page          (Next)
<BR>

The PSCF source code contains two different types of class templates,
which we refer to as "standard" and "divided" templates. They differ
in how files are organized, and in how they are treated by the compiler
and build system:

<ol type="A">
   <li>
   <b> Standard class templates </b> : These are C++ class templates
   that are designed to be implicitly instantiated. A preprocessor
   directive that includes the header for file for a standard class
   template must automatically include the definitions for all of its
   member functions.
   </li>

   <li>
   <b> Divided class templates </b>: These are class templates that
   are designed to be compiled only by explicit instantiation, for
   which the definitions of all non-inline member functions are placed
   in a separate file that is not included into the class header file.
   </li>

</ol>

The use of a divided class template is feasible for classes that will
only be used with only a small, known set of specializations, for which
it is practical to explicitly instantiate all required specializations.
In the PSCF code base, standard class templates are used throughout the
Util namespace, while divided templates are used for many of the class
templates defined in the Pscf namespace.

The simplest use of divided class templates in PSCF is for classes
that are designed for systems with periodic boundary conditions, and
for which the the dimension of space D is the only template parameter.
Specializations of such classes with D=1, D=2 and D=3 are always
explicitly instantiated and later linked to create executables.

By allowing template instantiations to be treated much like non-template
classes, the use of divided class templates in PSCF avoids some of the
most common disadvantages of templates in C++.  This usage signficantly
reduces compilation times of template heavy code base, because it
reduces the amount of source code that must be included files that
include the header for each class template.  It also tends to simplify
debugging of errors during development, because it breaks the build
process into smaller steps, often allows the compiler to generate more
easily interpretable error messages in response to syntax errors, The
cost is that a bit more thought is required to manage include directives,
and the need for explicit instantiation declarations.

\section developer_template_div_standard_sec A. Standard templates

Standard class templates are designed allow the safe use of implicit
instantiation.  All of the container class templates that are defined
in the src/util/containers directory, such as DArray and FSArray, are
standard templates.

The class definition for each standard class template is contained in a
header file that ends with file suffix .h. A preprocessor directive to
include this header file must also automatically include definitions of
any inline member functions. This requirement can be satisfied by
organizing the code for a standard class template in either of two ways:

   - Definitions of all class member functions may be placed in the
     class header file.

   - Definitions of non-inline class member functions may be placed in a
     separate template implementation file, with file name suffix .tpp
     that is included by the header file.

In the latter case, the .tpp file is normally included int the header
file by a include directive located at the botom of the header file.
A \*.tpp file for such a standard template should never be directly
included by any file other than the associated header file.
In this usage, the \*.tpp implementation file is treated by the compiler
as a part of the header file, and the separation into two files is
provided solely as convenience to human readers.

\section developer_template_div_divided_sec B. Divided class templates

The source code for the PSCF programs for periodic functions contains many
class templates for which there exist only a few valid choices of values
for the template parameters, and thus only a few possible specializations.
Most such classes are written as divided class templates, and compiled by
explicit instantiation.

Code required to use a divided class template generally involves the
following four components:

  - A class template definition

  - Definitions of class template member functions

  - Explicit instantiations declarations for specializations

  - Explicit instantiation definitions for all specializations

The class template definition (which contains member variables and
member function declarations) is always placed in a header file with
a base name given by the name of the class template followed by a
file name suffix .h. Definitions of non-inlined class template member
functions are often placed in a template implementation file with the
same base name and file name suffix .tpp.  Explicit instantiation
definitions for a class template are always placed in a source file
with a file name suffix .cpp (for pure C++ code) or .cu (for
C++ / CUDA code).  This source file is compiled by the build system
to create object code for relevant instantiations of the template.

To avoid compiler or linker errors, the organization of these four
components into files must satisfy the following requirements:

  - The class template definition is placed in a header (.h) file

  - A class template implementation (.tpp) file for a divided class
    template must include the corresponding header (.h) file.

  - A translation unit that contains explicit instantiation
    definitions for one or more specializations of the template
    must include the template implementation (.tpp) for the class
    template.

  - A translation unit containing source code in which an instance
    of a specialization of the class template is used in definition
    of different class or function must include the class template
    header (.h) file, and must contain an explicit instantiation
    declaration for that specialization.

In a source file that explicitly instantiations one or more
specializations of a class template, the include directive for the
template implementation (.tpp) file should normally be placed after
all include directives for header files, to allow for the
possibility that the template implementation file could refer to
class types that may defined in one or more of the header files.

Explicit instantiation declarations may be placed in different
locations depending on the nature of the class template.  In PSCF,
there are two primary cases:
<ol>
    <li>
    <b> Templates for which D is the only parameter </b>:
    If the only parameter of a class template is the integer
    dimension D of space, then the class template header file always
    contain explicit instantiation declarlations for the three
    physically relevant cases of D=1, D=2, and D=3.
    </li>

    <li>
    <b> Templates used only as base classes </b>:
    If a divided class template is only used to define base classes for
    a small number of derived classes, and different derived classes
    use different specializations of the base class template, then
    explicit instantiation declarations may sometimes be placed in the
    header files for the derived classes or derived class templates.
    </li>
</ol>
Case 1 is safe because the explicit instantiation declarations for
all relevant specializations of that template will be included into
any file that includes the header file.
Case 2 is safe if and only if we know that specializations of the
base class template are never instantiated in any context except
as base classes for a small number number of derived classes, and
each of those derived classes is defined in a header file that
contains explicit instantiation declarations for the specialization
of the base class template from which it is derived.

\subsection developer_template_div_divided_D_sub B.1: Templates for which D is the only parameter

Class templates for which the dimension of space D is the only template
parameter appear throughout the source code for periodic system that is
located in subdirectories src/rp, src/rpc, and src/prg.  Many of the
simplest cases occur in the src/rp directory.

As an example, consider the source code files associated with the class
template Pscf::Rp::Basis, for which the only template paramer is the
dimension of space, D.

The header file src/rp/crystal/Basis.h contains a definition of
class template Pscf::Rp::Basis, but does not contain definitions
of any non-inline member functions.  The key contents of this file,
in skeleton form, look like this:
\code
#ifndef PRDC_BASIS_H
#define PRDC_BASIS_H

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   // Class definition
   template <int D>
   class Basis {
      ...
   }

   // Inline member function definitions
   ...

   // External instantiation declarations
   extern template class Basis<1>;
   extern template class Basis<2>;
   extern template class Basis<3>;

}
}
#endif
\endcode
The template implementation file src/rp/crystal/Basis.tpp contains
the definitions of all non-inline member functions for the Basis class
template.  A skeleton version of this file (which is actually much
longer than the header file) looks something like this:
\code

#include "Basis.h"

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   // Non-inline member function definitions
   ....

}
}
\endcode
The compilable source file src/rp/crystal/Basis.cpp includes
Basis.tpp and explicit instantiation definitions for D=1, D=2, and
D=3:
\code
#include "Basis.tpp"

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   template class Basis<1>;
   template class Basis<2>;
   template class Basis<3>;

}
}
\endcode
The PSCF build system compiles the file src/rp/crystal/Basis.cpp,
places the resulting object code for the three specializations in a file
named Basis.o, and later incorporates the contents of this object file
into a static library file named rplib.a. The resulting library then
contains object code for three separate instantiations of the Basis
template with D=1, 2 and 3.

\subsection developer_template_div_divided_base_sub B.2. Templates Used Only as Base Classes

Class templates that are designed to be used only to construct base
classes for a small number of derived classes appear in several locations
within the PSCF source code:

   - **Solver templates**: Several class templates that are defined in
     the src/pscf/solvers directory are used to construct base classes for
     **solver** classes that are defined in each program-level directory.

   - **AM Iterator template**: The Pscf::AmIteratorTmpl defined in the
     src/pscf/iterators directory is used as a base class for classes
     that use some variant of the Anderson mixing algorithm to solve
     either the self-consistent field (saddle-point) equations or the
     partial saddle-point equations used in PS-FTS calculations.

   - **Templates for CPU and GPU class implementations**: Every class
     in the Pscf::Rp namespace, or in directory src/rp, is designed to
     be used as a base class for a pair of analogous classes defined in
     the Pscf::Rpc and Pscf::Rpg namespaces and used in the pscf_rpc
     CPU program and the pscf_rpg GPU program, respectively.

In each of these case, templates are used to avoid duplication of code
for closely analogous data structures or algorithms that are needed in
different programs or different contexts within a program.

In each of the cases described above, a base class template that is used
in more than one program is defined in a namespace that is accessible to
multiple programs (e.g., Pscf or Pscf::Rp), but specializations of this
template that are used in each program-level namespace end up using
arguments for one or more template type parameters which are classes that
are defined within the same program-level namespace. By convention, in
PSCF, names that are defined with a program-level namespace may not be
used outside of that namespace. This convention prohibits us from
providing explicit instantiation declarations in the header file for
a base class template that is defined outside any program-level
namespace for specializations that use type template arguments that
are defined within a program-level namespace. Instead, in these cases,
explicit instantiation declarations for specializations that are used
in only one program-level namespace are usually placed in a header file
for a derived class that is defined in that program-level namespace.
This usage is illustrated below with an example.

As an example, we consider the base class template Pscf::Rp::WFields .
Partial specializations of this template are used as base classes for
corresponding specializations of two derived class templates named
WFields that are defined in the Pscf::Rpc and Pscf::Rpc namespaces.
Each of these two WFields derived class templates has the integer D
as its only template parameter.  Within each of the two program-level
namespaces, Pscf::Rpc and Pscf::Rpg, specializations of WFields\<D\>
with D=1, 2, or 3 are used as containers for the chemical potential
fields (w-fields).

<b> Base class template (Rp::WFields) </b>

The Pscf::Rp::WFields base class template takes D as an integer
template parameter, and also takes 2 class name parameters that 
are denoted by RFT and FIT.  The definition of Rp::Rp::WFields 
given in the file src/rp/field/WFields.h is shown in skeleton form 
below:
\code
#ifndef RP_W_FIELDS_H
#define RP_W_FIELDS_H

// Header file includes
// Forward declarations

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   using namespace Util;
   using namespace Prdc;

   template <int D, class RFT, class FIT>
   class WFields
   {
      // Body of class definition

   };

   // Inline member functions

} // namespace Rp
} // namespace Pscf
#endif
\endcode
Within this template, the parameters RFT and FIT are aliases for classes 
that represent, respectively:

   -  RFT: a class that contains a real field defined on a mesh 
   -  FIT: a class that provides tools for field input and output

These two class names are treated as template parameters because
different classes are used for these purposes by pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg,
which differ in whether or not they use a GPU.

The argument of the RFT parameter must be a specialization of a class 
template named RField that is defined in the Pscf::Prdc::Cpu or 
Pscf::Prdc::Cuda namespace, and takes the dimension D of space as 
its only parameter, with D=1,2, or 3. A template specialization 
Pscf::Prdc::Cpu::RField\<D\> of the class template Pscf::Prdc::Cpu::Field 
is used in the definition of Pscf::Rpc::WFields\<D\>, which uses CPU 
memory, while a specialization Pscf::Prdc::Cuda::RField\<D\> is used 
in the definition of Pscf::Rpg::WFields\<D\>. which uses GPU memory.

The argument of the FIT parameter must be a specialization of a class
template named FieldIo that is defined in the Pscf::Rpc or Pscf::Rpg
namespace, and that takes the dimension D of space as its only parameter.
A template specialization Pscf::Rpc::FieldIo\<D\> is used in the definition
of Pscf::Rpc::WFields\<D\>, while a specialization Pscf::Rpg::FieldIo\<D\>
is used in the definition of Pscf::Rpg::WFields\<D\>, for D=1, 2 and 3.

Note that the header for the Rp::WFields base class template does not
contain any explicit instantiation declarations.  Declarations for all
required specializations of this template are instead placed in header
files for derived class templates, as discussed below.

The Pscf::Rp::WFields template has an associated template implementation
(.tpp) file. The contents of this file are shown in skeleton form below:
\code
#ifndef RP_W_FIELDS_TPP
#define RP_W_FIELDS_TPP

#include "WFields.h"

// Include directives for other header files

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

   // Definitions of non-inline member functions

}
}
#endif
\endcode
Note that the implementation (.tpp) file for a divided class template
includes the header (.h) file, as shown in this example. 

<b> Derived class template </b>

As an example of one of the two corresponding derived class templates, we
consider the template Pscf::Rpc::WFields, which is designed to use CPU
hardware. This is a class template for which the integer D is the only
template parameter. For each valid value of D=1, 2 or 3, a class template
specialization Pscf::Rpc::WFields\<D\> is derived from a specialization
of the base class template Pscf::Rp::WFields with the same value for
the integer parameter D, in which the two type parameters RFT and FIT
are assigned the following class name arguments:
\code
   RFT = Pscf::Prdc::Cpu::RField<D>
   FIT = Pscf::Rpc::FieldIo<D>
\endcode
The header file for the Pscf::Rpc::WFields derived class template is
shown in skeleton form below:
\code
#ifndef RPC_W_FIELDS_H
#define RPC_W_FIELDS_H

#include <rp/field/WFields.h>    // base class template header

// Other include directives
// Forward declarations

namespace Pscf {
namespace Rpc {

   using namespace Util;
   using namespace Prdc;
   using namespace Prdc::Cpu;

   // Class template definition
   template <int D>
   class WFields
     : public Rp::WFields<D, RField<D>, FieldIo<D> >
   {};

}
}

namespace Pscf {
   namespace Rp {
      // Explicit instantiation declarations - base class specializations
      using namespace Prdc;
      extern template class WFields<1, Cpu::RField<1>, Rpc::FieldIo<1> >;
      extern template class WFields<2, Cpu::RField<2>, Rpc::FieldIo<2> >;
      extern template class WFields<3, Cpu::RField<3>, Rpc::FieldIo<3> >;
   }
   namespace Rpc {
      // Explicit instantiation declarations - derived class specializations
      extern template class WFields<1>;
      extern template class WFields<2>;
      extern template class WFields<3>;
   }
}
#endif
\endcode

As shown in the above example, the header file for each derived class
template (e.g., Pscf::Rpc::WFields) includes explicit instantiation
declarations not only for the three valid specializations of the derived
class template (e.g., of Rpc::WFields\<D\> with D=1,2, and 3), but also
for the three corresponding specializations of the base class template
(e.g., of Rp::FieldIo). The declarations of relevant specializations of
the base class template are necessary to suppress implicit instantiation
of member functions that are defined by the base class template and
inherited by the derived class, which the compiler might otherwise
attempt to implicitly instantiate in a file that uses an instance of
a derived class specialization.

The header file for the class template Pscf::Rpg::FieldIo that is used
by the pscf_rpg program is very similar, except that it uses analogous
RField and FieldIo template specializations that are defined in namespaces
Pscf::Prdc::Cuda and Pscf::Rpg, designed to use a GPU.

The Pscf::Rpc::WFields template also has an associated source file
named src/rpc/field/WFields.cpp, which is compiled by the build system.
This file contains explicit instantiations definitions for relevant
specializations of both the derived class and the base class. The
main elements of this file looks like this:
\code
#include "WFields.h"               // derived class header
#include <rpc/field/FieldIo.tpp>
#include <pscf/cpu/VecOp.h>
#include <rp/field/WFields.tpp>    // base class implementation

// Explicit instantiation definitions
namespace Pscf {
   namespace Rp {
      using namespace Prdc;
      template class Rp::WFields<1, Cpu::RField<1>, Rpc::FieldIo<1> >;
      template class Rp::WFields<2, Cpu::RField<2>, Rpc::FieldIo<2> >;
      template class Rp::WFields<3, Cpu::RField<3>, Rpc::FieldIo<3> >;
   }
   namespace Rpc {
      template class WFields<1>;
      template class WFields<2>;
      template class WFields<3>;
   }
}
\endcode
Note that the implementation file for the Rp::WFields base class
template, referred to here as <rp/field/WFields.tpp>, must be included
in this source file because the compiler needs access to definitions of
all member functions to instantiate the three relevant specializations
of the base class template.

The source file src/rpc/fieldsWFields.h that is shown above contains
preprocessor include directives for two files that are used in the CPU
version of this class but not in the GPU version, referred to here as
rpc/field/FieldIo.tpp and pscf/cpu/VecOp.h.  Header files that are not
used by all specializations of the base class template are no included
within the base class template header or implementation file, but need
to be included here if they define types that are used in the base class
template member function definitions.  Specialized header files used in
derived class templates that are defined in Pscf::Rpc namespace are
often located in src/rpc, src/prdc/cpu, or src/pscf/cpu directories.
Corresponding specialized header files used by templates defined in
the Pscf::Rpg namespace are instead often located in the src/rpg,
src/prdc/cuda, or src/pscf/cuda directories.

The derived class template Pscf::Rpc::WFields that is used here as 
an example has associated header and source files, but no template
implementation file. A few class templates defined in the Pscf::Rpc and
Pscf::Rpg namespaces also have an associated template implementation
(.tpp) files. In these cases, this .tpp file contains definitions of 
member functions that are defined or overridden by the derived class 
template. For example, there exist template implementation files for 
the Pscf::Rpc::FieldIo and Pscf::Rpg::FieldIo classes. When such a 
.tpp file exists, it usually contains preprocessor include directives 
analogous to those shown above in the example source file, through 
which the .tpp file includes the derive class template header (.h) 
file, the base class template implementation (.tpp) file, and any 
required specialized header files. Including all these files into 
the .tpp file guarantees that they are accessible within the function 
definitions given in that file.  In this case, the .cpp or .cu source 
file can indirectly includes all of these files by including just 
that .tpp file.

The usage pattern described above relies on a convention that header
files for this type of divided base class template (e.g., Rp::FieldIo)
may never be directly included into any file other than a header file
for a derived class or derived class template.

<BR>
